//? Title: V-statistics: actual repeated-index tuples and plug-in variance
//? Subtitle: Actual native state and retained original experiments
//? Exports: both
//? Lang: en-GB

\abstract(
A kernel expectation under an empirical distribution includes repeated sample
indices. V-statistics average those ordered tuples, making the distinction from
distinct-index U-statistics explicit.
)

\toc

> The method

V_n=n^(−m)Σ_(i1,...,im)h(X_i1,...,X_im), with every index independently ranging
over0..n−1. For h(a,b)=(a−b)²/2, expanding the double sum gives
V_n=(1/n)Σ(X_i−mean)². Diagonal terms vanish, so V_n=((n−1)/n)U_n, where U uses
the unbiased variance denominator n−1. This is an exact finite-sample identity
for this kernel. Automatic mode enumerates up to5 million tuples, then
uses200000 Monte Carlo draws; a positive max_terms always requests that many
sampled tuples.

> Simple worked example

```{cpp, results="markdown", file="figures/v_statistic_tiny.svg", flags="-std=c++23 -O2 $(pkg-config --cflags --libs datamunge) -Wl,-rpath,$(pkg-config --variable=libdir datamunge)"}
#include <dm/dm.hpp>
int main() {
    namespace a = dm::algorithms;
    namespace g = dm::graphics;
    std::filesystem::create_directories("build");
    auto r =
        dm::stats::VStatistic(2).trace({1, 3},
                                       dm::stats::empirical_variance_kernel, 4);
    std::print("| actual indices | actual arguments | kernel value |\n|---|---|---:|\n");
    for (auto q : r.terms)
        std::print("|`{}`|`{}`|{}|\n", q.indices, q.arguments, q.value);
    std::print("\nActual mean {}; exact {}; calls {}.\n", r.result.estimate,
               r.result.exact, r.result.num_terms);
    g::Diagram concept_diagram(48, 1100);
    concept_diagram.node("n0", {0, 0}, "Sample1,3; degree2");
    concept_diagram.node("n1", {5, 0},
                         std::format("{} actual ordered tuples",
                                     r.result.num_terms));
    concept_diagram.node("n2", {10, 0},
                         std::format("Actual V variance {}", r.result.estimate));
    concept_diagram.edge("n0", "n1").edge("n1", "n2");
    concept_diagram.save_svg("build/v_statistic_tiny.svg");
}
```
